{"title":"2003 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Problems\">2003 AMC 12B #5</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2003 AMC 10B Problems\">2003 AMC 10B #6</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Many television screens are rectangles that are measured by the length of their diagonals. The ratio of the horizontal length to the height in a standard television screen is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/f/def7726c2babb527f7ab2ae4c436cc012ebbd351.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4:3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"32\" height=\"13\" />. The horizontal length of a \"<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/5/1051e7d41ec0b8bd688a872be17538413f4ee97b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$27$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />-inch\" television screen is closest, in inches, to which of the following?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/b/77bcb0fa3d35aa757337b6a71419731ddb78f974.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 20 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 20.5 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 21 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 21.5 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 22$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"426\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If you divide the television screen into two right triangles, the legs are in the ratio of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/f/8afd5df585afa29bc9aa6f75b7b7a308e79d4e92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 : 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"32\" height=\"13\" />, and we can let one leg be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/4/ed4977828049d99f20ccd3b4acb6bbd43c2eb70f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4x$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" /> and the other be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/e/70e634348f8df9eaeac4ced35db64d08a906532e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3x$\" width=\"19\" height=\"12\" />. Then we can use the Pythagorean Theorem.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/3/ae3feb4f3a5e87fd9f2395f803259581a80deddc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}(4x)^2+(3x)^2&amp;=27^2\\\\ 16x^2+9x^2&amp;=729\\\\ 25x^2&amp;=729\\\\ x^2&amp;=\\frac{729}{25}\\\\ x&amp;=\\frac{27}{5}\\\\ x&amp;=5.4\\end{align*}\" width=\"156\" height=\"183\" />\n</p><p>The horizontal length is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/3/c73a391dbb1d030d449c87475301a466068866be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5.4\\times4=21.6$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"110\" height=\"13\" />, which is closest to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/e/92ed595f64a705081a2c8ff76daf6fe36b9e6956.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) \\ } 21.5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"88\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>One can realize that the diagonal, vertical, and horizontal lengths all make up a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/7/827255dbd48aaf645900571ce04461a84082908a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3,4,5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" /> triangle. Therefore, the horizontal length, being the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/f/8afd5df585afa29bc9aa6f75b7b7a308e79d4e92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4 : 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"32\" height=\"13\" /> ratio, is simply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/d/4fd03d5b164ae8b40db27a6f8ddb20d9d43f95fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"11\" height=\"37\" /> times the hypotenuse. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/2/46264210dfb17560c5efaee5adc7a8de0e0c28a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot27=21.6 \\approx \\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 21.5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"205\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"2\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B\">2003 AMC 12B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_4\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 4\">Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_6\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 6\">Problem 6</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"2\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2003 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2003_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_3\" class=\"mw-redir